+# The Daily Grind
+
+A responsive coffee homepage with a native rewards registration form and a server-verified browser Email Verification Protocol (EVP) flow. No OTP or magic-link email is sent.
+
+## Deployment
+
+- Use Node.js 22.13 or later and install dependencies with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
+- Configure `APP_ORIGIN` as the exact HTTPS public origin (without a trailing slash), and set `NODE_ENV=production`.
+- Register that origin in Chrome's Email Verification origin trial. Set the issued token as `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`; the server adds the `Origin-Trial` response header to the homepage.
+- The runtime command is `pnpm start`. `PORT` defaults to `11222`.
+- Mount a persistent volume at `data/`; verified members are stored in `data/rewards.sqlite`. Keep this directory private and back it up. Run a single application instance: the short-lived form sessions and rate limits are in memory. For multiple instances, move these to a shared store.
+
+This is an experimental browser/provider integration. Customers need a supported browser, the feature enabled for this origin, and an active session with a participating email provider. A missing or invalid proof leaves the account uncreated and shows a retry message. The app never falls back to email delivery or silently treats the submitted address as verified.
+
+## Registration
+
+`GET /` creates a ten-minute, cryptographically random, single-use form nonce tied to an HttpOnly, SameSite cookie. In HTTPS deployments the cookie is also Secure. The nonce is rendered on the hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input. The form submits natively so the browser can inject the token during submission; JavaScript does not intercept it.
+
+`POST /api/rewards/signup` accepts the native URL-encoded form fields `firstName`, `lastName`, `email`, `evt`, `challenge`, and optional `marketing=yes`. The server checks the session, request origin and nonce, and consumes the nonce before asynchronous verification. It validates:
+
+- The email domain's DNS TXT delegation to the issuer.
+- The issuer's HTTPS metadata and public signing keys.
+- The issuer signature, verified email, expiry and issue time.
+- The browser signature, exact audience, nonce, freshness and SD-JWT digest binding.
+
+Provider fetches reject private addresses and redirects, pin DNS resolution to the HTTPS connection, and enforce time and response-size limits. The implementation supports the current zero-disclosure `EVT~KB-JWT` format, using ES256, EdDSA or Ed25519; it rejects other layouts. Successful verification creates a durable member record. Repeat registration does not alter existing members or marketing preferences. This implements registration, not a returning-member sign-in flow.
+
+## Checks
+
+`pnpm run build` performs syntax checks without executing the server. `pnpm test` runs generated-key cryptographic tests on the isolated verification module; it never imports the server entrypoint or starts a listener. No manual browser verification was performed.
+
+## References
+
+- [Chrome relying-party integration and origin trial](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification/verifier)
+- [Email Verification API specification](https://wicg.github.io/email-verification/)
+
+Photos are served from Unsplash; fonts from Google Fonts.
